{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794651338,"nonce":"bJRlyeE+0Bn6tuMWvzWg5v5gyc4=","title":"nAIR: compile Haxe to AIR directly from the typed AST, bypassing HashLink bytecode","message":"Goal: ash reads Haxe's typed AST and builds AIR from it, so the optimizer sees what the programmer wrote instead of what genhl left in the bytecode. When nAIR reaches conformance it becomes the default frontend; the HL-bytecode frontend stays for prebuilt .hl files.\n\n\n- Allocation: `new C(...)` hands the fresh object to its constructor, so SROA cannot scalarize it until the constructor is inlined (air/v2/mod.rs). The AST has the `new` and its arguments in one node.\n- Dispatch: class-hierarchy analysis on HL bytecode found 0 monomorphic sites of 3883 (memory note devirt-needs-a-guard). The AST has the hierarchy, `final`, and a closed world under `-dce full`.\n- Types: AIR cannot tell a pointer from a scalar (713cc00). The AST has `Null\u003cT\u003e`, generics before erasure, and the array element type.\n- Control flow and closures: loops, switches and captured-variable mutability are rebuilt by analysis today. The AST states them.\n- Exceptions: a Haxe try costs about 200 ns on wasm and unwinds through libunwind natively (b710dcc, c822ee5). The AST shows which tries can throw at all.\n\n\n- `--hl out.hl --no-output` writes no file, still defines `hl`, and still puts the HL std overrides on the classpath. A macro then sees the same typed program genhl would compile.\n- `Context.onGenerate` runs after DCE (hello world: 28959 -\u003e 27447 typed nodes); `onAfterTyping` runs before.\n- Hello world: 163 modules, about 27k nodes, 0.3 s. The full Haxe unit suite (compile-hl.hxml, -dce full): 3501 modules, 2609 classes, about 299k nodes. Stock genhl takes 2.7 s; the export-only run takes 4.1 s with a node counter that only walks the tree. A serializer will cost more.\n- Not yet known: what the filters change after these hooks (captured variables, exception wrapping, default arguments, local statics), whether `@:generic` instantiations are visible as separate classes, and macro time and memory on MBHaxe- or Ashui-sized programs.\n\n\nA. A Reflaxe compiler written in Haxe that lowers to AIR inside the macro. Reflaxe 4.0.0-beta is installed. The lowering and the later optimization work would run in the Haxe eval interpreter, where we cannot reuse our Rust passes or tests.\nB. A thin exporter macro in Haxe that serializes the typed AST and the types it uses to a versioned binary file, and a Rust crate that lowers that file to AIR. The lowering lives beside the passes it feeds, and golden files test it. The Zyntax tree has a Reflaxe-to-JSON typed-AST sample (reflaxe.zyntax/output/ForLoopTest.json); I could not find its sources, so I do not know how much of the exporter could be shared.\nC. A genair.ml generator in a forked Haxe compiler. It sees the post-filter AST and has no macro API limits. We would build and ship our own haxe binary and track upstream.\n\nRecommendation: B, written so the exporter can move into C if the macro API proves too thin. Phase 0 decides with numbers.\n\n\n1. nAIR is AIR v2 extended, not a new IR. The air crate does not depend on the VM (ModuleInfo, ModuleTables), and the walker, Cranelift, LLVM, direct wasm, OSR, inlining, GVN, LICM, SROA and the vectorizer already consume it. The frontend builds the module tables and implements ModuleInfo.\n2. The runtime ABI stays HashLink's. ash_std, hlwindow, hlwgpu, sdl, fmt and Heaps depend on hl_type, vdynamic, varray and vstring layouts. We bypass the bytecode, not the object model.\n3. New metadata rides on AIR: a value kind (713cc00), final and known-class receivers, non-null, purity, array bounds facts, closure-environment mutability, which tries can throw. Each lands with the pass that uses it.\n4. Positions: the walker and OSR transfer by position today (memory note interp-osr-air-coupling). nAIR defines positions over AIR, and serialize-to-bytecode stays only with the HL frontend.\n5. Standard library: compile the HL std (std/hl/_std) through the new frontend, so the same Haxe code and the same hlp_ natives run and ash_std does not change in phase 1. Replace pieces later where the AST lets us beat them (Array, String, StringBuf, Map).\n6. Both frontends coexist behind `--frontend hl|nair`, with a differential harness that builds a program both ways and compares output.\n\n\nException wrapping (haxe.Exception), captured variables and closure environments, optional and default arguments, local statics, string switch, enum encoding, virtual types for anonymous structures, Dynamic operators, `Null\u003cT\u003e` boxing, Array specialization (ArrayBytes and ArrayObj), type descriptors and reflection, static-init order, `@:hlNative` externs and `hl.*` types, positions for traces, and DCE interplay. This list comes from memory of genhl; Phase 0 replaces it with one read from genhl.ml and the filters in the 4.3.6 source.\n\n\n- P0 audit and decision: the exporter emits the typed AST for hello and the unit suite; audit genhl.ml and the filters; answer the unknowns above; choose A, B or C. Gate: the decision, with measurements, written here.\n- P1 subset: ints, floats, strings, arrays, classes, statics, no closures. Run on the walker, Cranelift and LLVM. Gate: hello, fib, nbody and the seven BM kernels give output identical to the .hl path.\n- P2 full language: enums, closures, try/catch, Dynamic, anonymous structures, generics, reflection, threads. Gate: unit, threads and sys on interp at 1069/1069 with no new SKIP, FAIL or TIMEOUT rows.\n- P3 every engine: tiers, OSR, AOT, wasm, phones. Gate: conformance on interp, AOT and wasm; the parity gate; MBHaxe, hlbox3d and Ashui run.\n- P4 optimization from the AST, one change at a time, each measured on the NUC against the .hl frontend: closed-world devirtualization, constructor SROA without inlining, closure inlining, `Null\u003cT\u003e` and null-check elimination, bounds-check elimination on array loops, a no-throw fast path for try. Targets: the rows we trail in c4f218b (spectralnorm, fasta, knucleotide) and 05169ba (binarytrees, knucleotide).\n- P5 default flip once P3 holds for consecutive CI runs. The .hl frontend stays for prebuilt .hl files and HDLL-only programs.\n\n\n- Macro time and memory on large programs.\n- The typed AST may lack what the filters add (decided in P0).\n- Haxe version coupling: 4.3.6 today, and the HL 2.0 and Haxe 5 work is deferred.\n- Two frontends double the test matrix until P5; the differential harness is the control.\n- HDLLs read class and type descriptors, so those must match genhl's layout exactly.\n\n\n- Does the HashLink runtime ABI stay (decision 2)?\n- Does the exporter ship in the ash haxelib (`haxe build.hxml`, then `ash build`), or does `ash` drive haxe itself?\n- Where is the reflaxe.zyntax source, and can the exporter be shared?\n- Is a forked Haxe compiler acceptable (option C)?","files":null}]}